WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.920
This island in the Indian Ocean supports
thriving seabird colonies and rich coral reefs

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:12.420
but nearby other islands are eerily silent.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:20.200
The cause of this difference lies in a complex web
of birds, rats, coral...and poop.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:28.140
The Chagos archipelago in the Indian Ocean
is home to over 60 tropical islands

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:34.660
nearly a third of which are completely free
from invasive species like rats or foxes.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:38.820
This safe haven for birds creates quite the cacophony.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.820
So here we are in the Chagos Archipelago

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.580
We're on a rat-free island,

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:46.100
there's never been rats on this particular island

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:50.120
There's a huge abundance and diversity of seabirds here

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:54.740
Terns, shearwaters, boobies, frigatebirds.

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:03.900
On neighboring islands, however, 
the arrival of sailing ships in the 1800s

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:08.040
brought an unwanted visitor - rats.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:11.220
The rodents bred like, well, rodents.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:17.600
They devoured seabird eggs and chicks
until they all but wiped out the bird populations.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.560
In contrast to this island where there's rats,

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:22.880
there's very, very few seabirds.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:26.220
The skies are empty and it's very quiet.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:38.520
But the story doesn't stop there, because the birds
play a critical role in the wider ecosystem

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:40.900
all thanks to their poop.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:44.580
Now these birds return to the islands where they roost

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:47.720
and they breed and they deposit huge amounts

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:50.560
of guano, or bird poo, on to the islands.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.900
And this guano is highly rich in nitrogen

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:56.700
So they're effectively fertilising the islands.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:03.220
This nitrogen is great for plants,
which thrive on the rat-free islands.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:07.680
But scientists wondered whether this 
aerial nitrogen delivery system

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:11.260
was also impacting the surrounding coral reefs.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:15.600
So we then went out into the shallow waters there

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:19.880
and sampled sponges that filter feed and macroalgae.

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:23.040
And those fish that are feeding on the algae out on the

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:25.860
reef crest, we were able to look at their growth rates.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:34.420
The team found that rat-free islands had two-and-a-half 
times more nitrogen across the entire ecosystem

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:42.860
and fish living on the nearby reefs grew faster and bigger than those around rat-infested islands.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:49.160
These fish graze on algae that otherwise covers the coral, slowing its growth.

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:55.760
The scientists observed three times more grazing
on the reefs surrounding rat-free islands.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:01.160
These healthier reefs may be more resilient 
to climate change.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:06.010
When it comes to interwoven ecosystems 
like those of the Chagos Archipelago

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:11.380
it seems invasive species like rats have
a bigger impact than we thought.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:16.860
So de-ratting oceanic islands in archipelagos
such as this

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:20.380
should really be a very high priority for conservation.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:23.540
It's not difficult to de-rat these islands.

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:26.840
and it would have a huge impact on both the terrestrial

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:28.560
environment and the adjacent marine environment.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:35.060
The team is now urging governments to consider 
investing in rat extermination programmes

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:37.840
as a way to save coral reefs.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:45.100
This unusual solution could  be a quick and 
cost-effective tool in the fight against coral decline

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:52.960
all due to a complex chain linking rats,
birds, fish, coral, and poop.

